::: {.doc-subject}

List the colliders of a directed graph: pairs of edges pointing into a common node.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L73275-L73356){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.colliders()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Colliders](../assets/graphs/colliders.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A causal graph in which talent and luck both point to success. The two arrows meeting head to head at success, all in the accent color, form a collider: two causes with a common effect. The card returns the one collider as the triple (talent, success, luck), effect in the middle." width="100%"}

A collider is a triple `(p1, c, p2)` with edges `p1 -> c` and `p2 -> c`: two causes
with a common effect. In causal diagrams colliders behave specially, since conditioning
on a collider (or one of its descendants) *creates* a dependence between its causes (see
`is_d_separator()`), the source of selection bias such as Berkson's paradox.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[tuple[int, int, int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Every collider `(p1, c, p2)` as node IDs, one per unordered pair of parents of each
    node.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is undirected.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Every pair of distinct in-neighbors of every node, $O(\sum_v \deg^-(v)^2)$. Matches
NetworkX's `dag.colliders` (and the deprecated `compute_v_structures`).

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~v_structures`) 

:   The colliders whose causes aren't adjacent.

[](`~moral_graph`) 

:   Join the parents of each node.

[](`~is_d_separator`) 

:   Check if a set of nodes d-separates two others.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

`A` and `B` both point into `C`, so `C` is a collider between them:

::: {#842e1551 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "C"), ("B", "C")], directed=True)
[[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in t] for t in graph.colliders()]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
[['A', 'C', 'B']]
```
:::
:::


### Where causes meet

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. It has 8 colliders. Six are at Blood pressure, which has four
causes:

::: {#43575cf0 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from collections import Counter

causal = ec.causal()
label = causal.get_label_from_id
found = causal.colliders()

Counter(label(middle) for _, middle, _ in found)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
Counter({'Blood pressure': 6, 'Weight': 1, 'Heart disease': 1})
```
:::
:::


Exercise and Diet meet only at Weight. They're independent, but among people of the same
weight, those who exercise less must eat better, so conditioning on a collider makes its
causes dependent:

::: {#af88a873 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight=[("Exercise", "Weight"), ("Diet", "Weight")],
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

